Q: Express JS and Mongo DB connection issues A: <p>As of my knowledge, <code>useNewUrlParser</code> is deprecated so instead you can use something like</p>
<pre><code>const mongoose = require('mongoose')
const connectDB = async () =&gt; {
  const db = await mongoose.connect(mongodb://localhost:27017/myDatabase)
  try {
    console.log(`Mongo DB connected in mongodb://${db.connection.host}:${db.connection.port}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(`Mongo DB not connected  ${err.stack}`);
  }
}

module.exports = connectDB also use the connectDB in your app.js/server.js
</code></pre>

Q: Gasless Transaction A: <p>Gasless transactions (where users don't pay gas fees) are now standardized through ERC-4337 Account Abstraction. Here's how it works:</p>
<p>The architecture:</p>
<p>• Smart Account — User's wallet is a smart contract, not an EOA<br />
• Bundler — Aggregates &quot;user operations&quot; into regular transactions<br />
• Paymaster — Third-party contract that pays gas on behalf of users</p>
<p>Implementation approach:</p>
<p>You have two options:</p>
<p>Option A: Build from scratch</p>
<p>1. Deploy an ERC-4337 smart account factory<br />
2. Run or connect to a bundler<br />
3. Deploy a paymaster contract with funds<br />
4. Build the client SDK to create UserOperations</p>
<p>Option B: Use existing infrastructure</p>
<pre><code>import { useOpenfort, ConnectButton } from &quot;@openfort/react&quot;;  

function App() {  
  const { sendTransaction } = useOpenfort();  

  const handleTransfer = async () =\&gt; {  
    // Gas is sponsored automatically by the configured paymaster  
    await sendTransaction({  
      to: &quot;0x...&quot;,  
      data: contractInterface.encodeFunctionData(&quot;transfer&quot;, \[recipient, amount\])  
    });  
  };  

  return (  
    \&lt;\&gt;  
      \&lt;ConnectButton /\&gt;  
      \&lt;button onClick={handleTransfer}\&gt;Send (Gasless)\&lt;/button\&gt;  
    \&lt;/\&gt;  
  );  
}  
</code></pre>
<p>The user signs in with email/social, gets a smart account, and all transactions are gas-sponsored. No ETH required.</p>
<p>Key resources:</p>
<p>• ERC-4337 spec (<a href="https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-4337" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-4337</a>)<br />
• Openfort gas sponsorship docs (<a href="https://openfort.io/docs/configuration/gas-sponsorship" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://openfort.io/docs/configuration/gas-sponsorship</a>)</p>

Q: How can I apply a streaming parameter from Java into an Oracle BLOB? A: <p>Similar to the question <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/17312133/1509264">How to create BLOB object?</a>, use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/sql/Connection.html#createBlob--" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Connection.createBlob()</code></a> to create a <code>Blob</code> object and then use <code>Blob.setBinaryStream()</code> and assign the <code>Blob</code> to the prepared statement:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try (
  InputStream is = new FileInputStream(&quot;photos/photo1.png&quot;);
  Connection conn = getConnection();
  PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(
    &quot;SELECT id FROM person WHERE photo = ?&quot;
  );
  Blob blob = conn.createBlob();
  OutputStream os = blob.setBinaryStream(1);
) {
  byte[] buffer = new byte[10 * 1024];
  int nread = 0;

  while ((nread = is.read(buffer)) != -1) {
    os.write(buffer, 0, nread);
  }

  ps.setBlob(1, blob);
  try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
    while (rs.next()) {
      // do something
    }
  }
  blob.free();
}
</code></pre>
